pears only in (0,0). So a must be 0. So a=0.\n\nNow c4: d,a allowed: (0,0), (1,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1). Since a=0, allowed pairs with a=0 are (0,0) only? Check: (0,0) matches (d=0,a=0). (0,1) has a=1, not 0. So only (0,0) works. So d=0.\n\nThus d=0.\n\nNow c10: d,g allowed: (0,2) or (1,0). Since d=0, allowed pair is (0,2). So g=2.\n\nThus g=2.\n\nNow c7: g,h allowed: g=2, allowed pairs with g=2: (2,0) only? Let's check c7 allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,3), (0,2), (0,1), (1,2). So g=2 appears only in (2,0). So h=0.\n\nThus h=0.\n\nNow c8: h,e allowed: h=0, e=2. Check allowed pairs: (0,2) is present. So c8 satisfied (h=0,e=2) is allowed.\n\nThus far consistent.\n\nNow we have e=2, h=0.\n\nNow c5: e,f allowed: e=2, allowed pairs with e=2: (2,2) and (2,0). So f can be 2 or 0.\n\nThus f ∈ {2,0}.\n\nNow c6: f,g allowed: g=2. Allowed pairs with g=2: from c6 allowed pairs: (2,2) (f=2,g=2), (0,0) (f=0,g=0) not match, (3,2) (f=3,g=2), (0,3) (f=0,g=3) not, (3,3) (f=3,g=3). So allowed (f,g) pairs that have g=2 are (2,2) and (3,2). So f must be 2 or 3.\n\nThus f must satisfy both c5 and c6 constraints: f ∈ {2,0} from c5, and f ∈ {2,3} from c6. Intersection is f=2.\n\nThus f=2.\n\nNow we have f=2.\n\nCheck c5: (e=2,f=2) is allowed (2,2) yes.\n\nNow c6: (f=2,g=2) is allowed (2,2) yes.\n\nNow we have b=0, a=0, d=0, g=2, h=0, e=2, f=2.\n\nNow we need to assign c.\n\nc2: b,c allowed: b=0, allowed pairs: (0,1) and (0,0). So c can be 1 or 0.\n\nc3: c,d allowed: d=0. Allowed pairs with d=0: from c3 allowed: (1,0) (c=1,d=0) and (3,0) (c=3,d=0). Also (2,2) not, (1,1) not, (3,1) not. So c must be 1 or 3.\n\nThus c must satisfy both c2 and c3: from c2, c ∈ {0,1}. From c3, c ∈ {1,3}. Intersection is c=1.\n\nThus c=1.\n\nCheck c2: (b=0,c=1) allowed yes (0,1). c3: (c=1,d=0) allowed yes (1,0). Good.\n\nNow all variables assigned:\n\na=0\nb=0\nc=1\nd=0\ne=2\nf=2\ng=2\nh=0\n\nLet's verify all constraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) present.\n\nc2: (b,c) = (0,1) allowed? Yes (0,1) present.\